export const DATA_57: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-074", num: 74, title: "Đề 074", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Artificial Intelligence in Everyday Life", paragraphs: ["Artificial intelligence, often called AI, is no longer something that belongs only to science fiction movies or high-tech laboratories. Today, AI has become a quiet but powerful part of our daily lives. From the moment we wake up and check our phones to the time we go to sleep, AI is working in the background to make our routines easier, faster, and more convenient. Most people use AI every day without even realizing it.", "One of the most common ways people interact with AI is through voice assistants. Tools like virtual assistants on smartphones can answer questions, set reminders, play music, and even control smart home devices. These assistants use a type of AI called natural language processing, which allows computers to understand and respond to human speech. A few years ago, talking to a machine felt strange and unreliable. Today, millions of people ask their voice assistants for directions, recipes, and even advice on what to wear based on the weather.", "Another area where AI plays a huge role is in online shopping and entertainment. When you visit a shopping website, the site uses AI to study your browsing and buying habits. It then recommends products that it thinks you will like. Similarly, video streaming platforms use AI to suggest movies and shows based on what you have already watched. This personalized experience makes it easier for users to find content they enjoy, but it also means that companies are constantly collecting data about our preferences and behavior.", "AI is also changing the way we communicate. Many email services now use AI to filter out spam and sort messages into different folders automatically. Some messaging apps offer smart reply suggestions — short responses that the app predicts you might want to send based on the message you received. Grammar-checking tools powered by AI help writers correct mistakes and improve the clarity of their writing in real time. These small conveniences add up to save people a significant amount of time throughout the day.", "In the field of healthcare, AI is beginning to assist doctors and nurses with important tasks. AI programs can analyze medical images such as X-rays and scans to help identify possible health problems more quickly than a human doctor might. AI can also remind patients to take their medicine on time or help track symptoms over a period of time. While AI is not yet ready to replace medical professionals, it is becoming a valuable tool that supports better and faster decision-making in hospitals and clinics.", "Despite all its benefits, AI also raises important questions about privacy and fairness. When AI systems collect personal data to improve their recommendations, users may not always know how their information is being used. Additionally, AI systems are only as fair as the data they are trained on. If the data contains biases, the AI may produce unfair or inaccurate results. It is important for both governments and technology companies to work together to create rules that ensure AI is used responsibly and for the benefit of everyone.", "In conclusion, artificial intelligence has already become an invisible but essential part of modern life. It helps us shop, communicate, stay healthy, and entertained. As AI continues to develop and improve, its role in our daily lives will only grow larger. Understanding how AI works and how it affects us is no longer optional — it is something every person should be aware of in today's rapidly changing world."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Voice assistants are the most useful form of artificial intelligence available today.", "People are afraid of artificial intelligence because it collects personal data.", "Artificial intelligence has become an important part of people's daily lives in many different areas.", "AI is mostly used in hospitals and medical clinics to help doctors."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what technology allows voice assistants to understand human speech?", options: ["Smart home automation", "Natural language processing", "Data filtering systems", "Personalized recommendation engines"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a way AI helps people communicate more efficiently?", options: ["AI suggests short replies based on messages that have been received.", "AI sends messages on behalf of users without their input.", "AI rewrites emails to make them more professional.", "AI translates messages between different languages automatically."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["virtual tools", "voice assistants", "millions of people", "smartphone companies"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how is AI currently being used in healthcare?", options: ["AI is fully replacing doctors in diagnosing and treating patients.", "AI is training new medical students through online courses.", "AI is helping to analyze medical images and remind patients about medication.", "AI is writing medical reports and prescriptions for doctors."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"recommends\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["suggests", "demands", "purchases", "removes"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern does the author raise about AI systems?", options: ["AI systems often make mistakes when processing voice commands.", "AI systems are replacing human workers in most industries.", "AI systems can produce unfair results if they are trained on biased data.", "AI systems are too expensive for most companies to use."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an area where AI is used in everyday life?", options: ["Email filtering and messaging", "Online shopping and entertainment", "Healthcare and medical imaging", "Foreign language translation services"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"essential\" in the final paragraph is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unnecessary", "outdated", "extremely important", "easily noticed"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the future of AI in daily life?", options: ["AI development will slow down because most problems have already been solved.", "AI will only be used in specialized industries like medicine and banking.", "AI will continue to grow and play an even bigger role in people's lives.", "AI will soon be banned due to concerns about privacy."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Surprising History of the Pop It Toy", paragraphs: ["Few toys have captured global attention as quickly and unexpectedly as the Pop It. With its bright colors, satisfying clicking sounds, and simple silicone design, the toy became a worldwide sensation almost overnight. However, the story behind the Pop It is far older and more complex than most people realize. Its origins stretch back several decades, rooted in both personal tragedy and creative vision.", "The Pop It was originally invented by a couple named Theo and Ora Coster, Israeli game designers who were well-known in the board game industry. Ora Coster came up with the concept in the 1970s, inspired by a vision she had after the death of her sister from breast cancer. In her vision, she imagined pressing bubbles on the chest of a woman — a deeply emotional image that she later transformed into a physical toy concept. The couple eventually patented the idea, but for decades, the design sat largely unused because they could not find a manufacturer willing to produce it at a reasonable cost.", "For many years, the toy existed only as a prototype and a patent. The Coster family tried repeatedly to bring it to market, but the project never gained enough financial backing. It was not until after Theo Coster's death that his children discovered the old patent and decided to revive it . They partnered with a Canadian toy company called Buffalo Games, which helped design a more polished version of the toy. The product was officially launched around 2019, but it did not immediately attract widespread attention.", "The real turning point for the Pop It came during the global pandemic in 2020 and 2021. As millions of people around the world found themselves stuck at home, demand for simple, tactile toys and stress-relief products skyrocketed. Videos of people playing with Pop Its began spreading rapidly on social media platforms, particularly among younger audiences. The satisfying sound of each bubble being pressed, and the ability to flip the toy over and start again, made it endlessly engaging. Within months, the Pop It had become one of the best-selling toys in the world.", "Manufacturers quickly responded to the surge in demand by producing Pop Its in an enormous variety of shapes, sizes, and colors. What began as a simple square or circle soon expanded to include dinosaurs, unicorns, pizza slices, and hundreds of other creative forms. The toy was also produced in different materials, offering varying levels of resistance and sound. This wide range of options meant that the Pop It could appeal to children and adults alike, from toddlers who enjoyed the motor activity to office workers using it as a desk stress toy.", "Beyond its commercial success, some researchers and educators began to take a closer look at the possible benefits of the Pop It. Occupational therapists noted that the repetitive pressing motion could help children with sensory processing differences, anxiety, or attention difficulties. The toy was sometimes compared to fidget spinners, which had a similar spike in popularity a few years earlier, though the Pop It appeared to offer a wider range of textures and interactions. Some schools allowed students to use the toy quietly during class as a focus aid, though others banned it due to the noise it produces.", "The story of the Pop It is, in many ways, a story about timing and opportunity. A concept born from grief, shelved for decades due to lack of interest, and then propelled to global fame by a worldwide health crisis is remarkable in any industry. It also highlights how social media can transform an ordinary product into a cultural phenomenon almost overnight. Whether the Pop It's popularity will prove lasting or whether it will fade like many toy trends before it remains to be seen. What is certain is that it has already left a mark on both the toy industry and popular culture."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To trace the origin and rise to global popularity of the Pop It toy.", "To compare the Pop It with other popular toys like the fidget spinner.", "To argue that the Pop It toy is more beneficial than other stress-relief products.", "To explain how social media companies profit from toy trends."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what originally inspired Ora Coster to create the Pop It concept?", options: ["A business meeting with a Canadian toy manufacturer", "A request from children in her community for a new type of toy", "Her experience working as an occupational therapist", "A vision she had following the death of her sister"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Theo Coster's business career", "the old patent and original toy design", "the toy company Buffalo Games", "the financial backing from investors"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what caused the Pop It to become massively popular around 2020 and 2021?", options: ["The toy's price dropped significantly, making it affordable for more families.", "A famous celebrity was seen using it in a television advertisement.", "People staying home during the global pandemic sought simple, stress-relieving toys.", "The toy was featured in an international educational program."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did manufacturers respond to the growing demand for Pop Its?", options: ["They focused exclusively on producing the original square and circle shapes.", "They created Pop Its in a wide variety of shapes, sizes, and materials.", "They partnered with schools to distribute the toys as educational tools.", "They reduced the number of colors available to lower production costs."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which group of professionals noted potential benefits of the Pop It for certain children?", options: ["Occupational therapists", "Child psychologists", "Educational researchers", "Pediatric nurses"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason some people use the Pop It toy?", options: ["As a focus aid for students during class", "As a stress-relief tool for adults at their desks", "As a sensory support for children with anxiety", "As a physical therapy tool to strengthen hand muscles"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"tactile\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["related to sight and visual perception", "related to sound and hearing", "related to memory and mental activity", "related to touch and physical sensation"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"propelled\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["discovered by chance", "driven forward powerfully", "carefully planned in advance", "slowed down considerably"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of the Pop It's future popularity?", options: ["The author believes the Pop It will quickly disappear like all toy trends.", "The author is uncertain whether the Pop It's success will last, but acknowledges its cultural impact.", "The author thinks social media will cause the Pop It to become even more popular in future years.", "The author is confident the Pop It will remain popular for many decades."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Societal Shifts in Family Planning: From One Child to Two", paragraphs: ["For more than three decades, China's One Child Policy stood as one of the most ambitious and controversial population management experiments in modern history. Introduced in 1980 by the Chinese government under Deng Xiaoping's administration, the policy was designed to curb rapid population growth that officials feared would overwhelm the country's limited economic resources. Families who complied were rewarded with financial incentives and better access to healthcare and education, while those who violated the restrictions faced fines, job loss, and in some extreme cases, forced sterilization. The policy succeeded in dramatically slowing China's birth rate, but it also set in motion a series of profound demographic and social consequences that would take generations to fully understand.", "One of the most immediate effects of the policy was a significant shift in family structure. The traditional Chinese family, which had historically valued large households and multiple children, was forced to adapt to a single-child norm. [1] This transformation was not merely numerical. It fundamentally altered the dynamics of parenting, sibling relationships, and the distribution of family resources. Children born under the policy, sometimes called \"little emperors\" by sociologists, were often the sole recipients of the emotional and financial investment of two parents and four grandparents. [2] Research conducted in subsequent decades revealed that while these children tended to receive greater educational opportunities, they sometimes struggled with social skills associated with sharing, negotiation, and peer collaboration — qualities typically developed through sibling interaction.", "The gender dimension of the policy proved particularly troubling. In a society where sons were traditionally preferred for their role in carrying on the family name and providing financial support in old age, the restriction to one child led many families to pursue sex-selective practices. [3] Official statistics from the early 2000s recorded some of the most imbalanced sex ratios at birth in recorded history, with certain provinces reporting as many as 120 male births for every 100 female births. Demographers warned that this surplus of men — often referred to as the \"bare branches\" generation — could lead to increased social instability, rising crime rates, and long-term marriage market disruptions. [4]", "By the early 2010s, it had become increasingly clear to Chinese policymakers that the demographic costs of the One Child Policy were unsustainable. The country was aging rapidly, with a shrinking working-age population being asked to support a growing proportion of elderly citizens. Economists projected that without intervention, China's labor force would contract significantly, threatening the country's continued economic expansion. In 2013, the government introduced a partial relaxation, allowing couples in which one partner was an only child to have two children. This adjustment, however, produced fewer new births than anticipated, suggesting that the policy itself was not the only factor shaping couples' reproductive decisions.", "The full transition to the Two Child Policy came in 2015, when China officially announced that all married couples could have two children. The move was widely seen as an acknowledgment that the One Child Policy had run its course and that a new demographic direction was urgently needed. Yet the results were again more modest than the government had hoped. Birth rates did increase briefly in 2016, reflecting a rush among couples who had previously delayed having a second child. However, the numbers quickly leveled off and began declining once more. Surveys conducted among young urban couples revealed a complex web of reasons for the reluctance to have more children, including rising housing costs, expensive childcare, demanding work schedules, and shifting cultural values around personal freedom and career ambition.", "The sociological implications of this reluctance extend beyond simple economics. Scholars argue that the One Child Policy did not merely reduce birth numbers — it actively reshaped the aspirations and identities of an entire generation. Women who grew up as only children often internalized values of independence and self-sufficiency that made the prospect of raising multiple children feel incompatible with their professional and personal goals. Men, similarly, had grown up without the experience of sharing parental attention, which some researchers suggest may have influenced their expectations of family life. The policy, in other words, changed not just how many children Chinese families had, but how they thought about children and family responsibility altogether.", "In 2021, China took yet another step, announcing a Three Child Policy in response to continued declining birth rates. However, demographic experts cautioned that policy changes alone are insufficient to reverse deeply embedded social norms and economic pressures. Countries such as South Korea, Japan, and Singapore have spent billions of dollars on pro-natalist incentives with only marginal success, suggesting that once a society has culturally adapted to small family sizes, reversing that trend through government policy alone is extraordinarily difficult. China's ongoing experience with population management thus offers a powerful case study in the unintended consequences of demographic engineering — a lesson with relevance far beyond its own borders."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The role of women's education in naturally reducing birth rates across East Asian societies.", "The broad social, demographic, and cultural consequences of China's shift from a one-child to a two-child family planning policy.", "The political debates within the Chinese government that led to the introduction of the One Child Policy.", "The economic rivalry between China and other Asian nations over population growth policies."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why were children born under the One Child Policy sometimes called \"little emperors\"?", options: ["They were given leadership roles in their schools and communities from a young age.", "They demonstrated exceptional academic achievement compared to children in other countries.", "They were the exclusive focus of the emotional and financial investment of both parents and grandparents.", "They were raised with stricter discipline than children in earlier generations."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on information from both paragraphs 1 and 4, which of the following best describes an unintended consequence of the One Child Policy?", options: ["The policy successfully prevented economic collapse by reducing strain on public resources.", "The policy created a demographic imbalance that ultimately threatened the same economic stability it was designed to protect.", "The policy encouraged more Chinese families to move from rural areas to cities in search of better opportunities.", "The policy produced a generation of highly independent workers who drove China's economic growth."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did official statistics from the early 2000s reveal about China's birth patterns?", options: ["Birth rates were highest in rural provinces where enforcement of the policy was weakest.", "The total birth rate had fallen to the lowest level ever recorded in Chinese history.", "Female births had outnumbered male births in most provinces for the first time.", "Some provinces recorded as many as 120 male births for every 100 female births."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the outcome of the 2013 partial relaxation of the One Child Policy?", options: ["It led to a sharp decline in the number of couples choosing to marry.", "It was quickly reversed after public opposition from urban residents.", "It produced fewer new births than the government had predicted.", "It triggered a significant and sustained increase in China's birth rate."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason why young urban couples were reluctant to have more children?", options: ["Demanding work schedules", "Insufficient government financial support for families", "Shifting cultural values around personal freedom", "Rising housing costs"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What conclusion can be drawn by combining information from paragraphs 5 and 6 about why the Two Child Policy did not achieve the expected results?", options: ["Young couples were influenced not only by financial pressures but also by deeply reshaped values and identities formed during the one-child era.", "Rising divorce rates among younger generations made the prospect of raising two children less practical.", "The policy failed primarily because the government did not promote it adequately through media campaigns.", "The Two Child Policy was undermined by continued enforcement of the original one-child restrictions in rural areas."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["researchers who studied the effects of the policy", "women who grew up as only children", "Chinese families as a whole", "government policymakers who designed the restrictions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the author imply in the final paragraph about the effectiveness of government pro-natalist policies?", options: ["Once a society has adapted culturally to small family sizes, government policy alone is rarely sufficient to reverse the trend.", "Pro-natalist policies are most effective when they are introduced before cultural norms around family size have fully formed.", "China's Three Child Policy is likely to succeed where those of South Korea and Japan have failed because of its unique economic conditions.", "Financial incentives from governments are the primary reason birth rates recover in countries that have experienced population decline."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"This concentration of resources and affection, while beneficial in some respects, was not without its psychological and social drawbacks.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Digital Dilemma: The Cultural and Economic Impact of Online Piracy", paragraphs: ["The proliferation of high-speed internet access across the globe has fundamentally altered the way creative works are produced, distributed, and consumed. [1] While this digital revolution has democratized access to art, music, film, and literature on an unprecedented scale, it has simultaneously given rise to one of the most persistent and economically significant challenges facing creative industries: online piracy. The unauthorized reproduction and distribution of copyrighted material — once constrained by the physical limitations of cassette tapes and bootleg DVDs — has metastasized into a virtually frictionless global network of illicit file-sharing, streaming, and downloading that generates no revenue for the creators whose work it disseminates.", "Estimating the precise financial damage caused by digital piracy is a notoriously contentious exercise. Industry groups have historically cited figures in the hundreds of billions of dollars annually, though independent economists have often challenged these calculations as methodologically flawed. The central dispute concerns the so-called \"displacement assumption\" — the premise that each pirated copy represents a lost sale. Critics of this approach argue that many individuals who access pirated content would not have purchased it at market price regardless, meaning that the actual revenue loss is substantially lower than industry estimates suggest. [2] Conversely, some researchers contend that piracy functions as a form of free advertising, particularly for emerging artists who lack the marketing budgets to reach new audiences through conventional channels. The economic reality, most analysts concede, likely lies somewhere between these competing positions.", "Beyond the financial dimension, online piracy raises complex questions about cultural access and equity. In many lower-income countries, the pricing structures of major streaming platforms and digital retailers are effectively prohibitive for large segments of the population, even when adjusted for local purchasing power. [3] For these consumers, piracy may represent the only practical means of accessing global cultural content — films, music, software, and academic texts — that wealthier populations take for granted. This dynamic complicates the moral framing of piracy as straightforwardly theft, positioning it instead within a broader discourse about the commodification of knowledge and the unequal global distribution of cultural participation. The question of whether intellectual property frameworks designed primarily in and for wealthy economies adequately serve the needs of a diverse global population remains deeply unresolved.", "The response of the creative industries to digital piracy has evolved considerably over the past two decades. Initial strategies relied heavily on litigation and aggressive enforcement — most infamously illustrated by the legal actions brought against individual file-sharers in the early 2000s, a campaign that generated significant public backlash without meaningfully reducing piracy rates. This period of confrontation gradually gave way to a more pragmatic approach: if piracy thrives where legal alternatives are inadequate, the solution may lie in making legitimate options sufficiently convenient and affordable. The extraordinary commercial success of platforms such as Spotify, Netflix, and Steam is frequently cited as evidence that consumers will pay for content when the user experience is seamless and the price point is reasonable. [4]", "Yet the rise of legal streaming has not eliminated piracy — it has, in certain respects, restructured it. The fragmentation of streaming services, each holding exclusive rights to different libraries of content, has recreated the very inconvenience that platforms originally promised to resolve. Consumers who wish to access a wide variety of content must now subscribe to multiple services at a combined cost that frequently exceeds what they once paid for a single cable package. Research conducted by several digital media analysts has found a strong correlation between the growth of subscription fragmentation and a corresponding uptick in piracy rates, particularly for films and television series whose rights are distributed across competing platforms. The industry, it seems, has inadvertently reconstructed the market conditions that originally drove consumers toward unauthorized alternatives.", "The legal frameworks governing intellectual property in the digital age have struggled to keep pace with the speed of technological change. Copyright law, largely conceived in an era of physical media, operates on principles of territorial jurisdiction and fixed reproduction that sit uneasily with the borderless, instantaneous nature of digital distribution. Efforts to strengthen enforcement — including the controversial provisions of legislative proposals such as the Stop Online Piracy Act in the United States and similar measures in the European Union — have consistently provoked fierce resistance from civil liberties advocates, who warn that overly broad anti-piracy legislation poses significant risks to freedom of expression and open internet access. The tension between protecting creators' rights and preserving an open digital commons remains one of the defining policy debates of the early twenty-first century.", "What emerges from a careful examination of online piracy is not a simple narrative of criminal behavior versus rightful ownership, but rather a multifaceted collision of economic incentives, cultural values, technological capability, and legal philosophy. Creative industries that frame piracy exclusively through the lens of loss may be missing the opportunity to engage with the deeper structural questions that sustain it. A more productive approach, some scholars argue, would involve rethinking the global pricing and distribution models that make piracy a rational choice for millions of consumers — not by condoning theft, but by honestly confronting the conditions that make it persistently appealing."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the author's approach to the topic of online piracy throughout the passage?", options: ["The author argues that the creative industries are primarily responsible for the persistence of piracy due to their pricing strategies.", "The author presents piracy as a complex phenomenon shaped by intersecting economic, cultural, and legal forces rather than a simple moral binary.", "The author condemns online piracy as an unambiguous form of theft that causes irreparable harm to creative industries.", "The author advocates for full decriminalization of piracy on the grounds that it promotes cultural equity."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"metastasized\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["transformed into a legitimate and regulated form of digital distribution", "been contained within specific geographic regions by international copyright agreements", "spread aggressively and pervasively into a much larger and more harmful form", "gradually declined in influence due to improved legal enforcement"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the author imply by stating in paragraph 2 that \"the economic reality likely lies somewhere between these competing positions\"?", options: ["The author suggests that neither the industry's inflated loss estimates nor the claim that piracy functions as free advertising fully captures the economic truth.", "The author implies that independent economists are more trustworthy than industry groups when assessing financial data.", "The author believes the entertainment industry's financial losses from piracy are largely exaggerated and should be disregarded.", "The author concludes that piracy has a net positive effect on the economy because it introduces new consumers to artists' work."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the outcome of the creative industries' initial legal strategy against piracy in the early 2000s?", options: ["It successfully deterred casual pirates while having little effect on organized criminal networks.", "It resulted in a landmark international agreement that significantly reduced global piracy rates.", "It prompted major file-sharing platforms to voluntarily shut down and seek licensing agreements.", "It generated considerable public backlash while failing to produce a meaningful reduction in piracy."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"displacement assumption\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the claim that streaming platforms have displaced traditional broadcast channels as the main venue for piracy", "the argument that digital piracy has displaced physical media as the primary driver of revenue loss", "the premise that every instance of piracy directly corresponds to a sale that would otherwise have occurred", "the theory that pirated content forces paying consumers to switch to cheaper subscription services"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what unintended consequence has the fragmentation of streaming services produced?", options: ["It has recreated the inconvenience and high combined costs that originally motivated consumers to seek pirated content.", "It has driven many independent artists to abandon streaming platforms in favor of direct-to-consumer sales.", "It has led to a consolidation of the streaming market as smaller platforms merge with larger competitors.", "It has caused streaming platforms to invest less in original content due to reduced subscription revenue."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT identified as a factor that sustains consumer demand for pirated content?", options: ["Prohibitively high pricing in lower-income countries", "The inconvenience created by content being split across multiple platforms", "A general moral indifference among younger consumers toward intellectual property rights", "Inadequate legal alternatives in terms of convenience and affordability"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What tension does the author describe in paragraph 6 regarding legislative anti-piracy measures?", options: ["The challenge of updating copyright law quickly enough to address new forms of piracy before they become widespread.", "The disagreement between the film industry and the music industry over which sector should receive priority protection under copyright law.", "The conflict between national governments and multinational technology companies over jurisdiction of digital enforcement.", "The difficulty of balancing creators' intellectual property rights against the risk that broad enforcement laws may threaten freedom of expression and open internet access."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in the final paragraph refers to _______.", options: ["the legal philosophy governing digital intellectual property", "the structural critique raised by scholars of the creative industries", "the global pricing and distribution model used by streaming platforms", "online piracy as a practice"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"This conclusion, if accurate, would significantly undermine the justification for aggressive litigation as the primary tool of enforcement.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-118", num: 118, title: "Đề 118", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Coffee: From Ancient Origins to Everyday Ritual", paragraphs: ["Few beverages have shaped human culture as profoundly as coffee. Today, more than two billion cups are consumed worldwide every single day, making it one of the most traded commodities on the planet after oil. Yet despite its global dominance, coffee's journey from a wild plant growing on Ethiopian hillsides to the steaming cup enjoyed in offices, homes, and cafés around the world is a story filled with fascinating twists, cultural transformations, and even political controversy.", "The story of coffee is generally believed to have begun in the highlands of Ethiopia, where, according to popular legend, a goat herder named Kaldi noticed that his animals became unusually energetic after eating the red berries from a certain tree. Curious, he brought these berries to a local monastery, where monks reportedly made a drink from them and discovered that it kept them alert during long evening prayers. While historians debate whether this tale is true, what is well documented is that coffee cultivation and trade began on the Arabian Peninsula around the fifteenth century. By the sixteenth century, coffeehouses known as \"qahveh khaneh\" had appeared across the Middle East, Persia, and Turkey. These were not merely places to drink coffee — they were vibrant social hubs where people gathered to exchange news, play chess, and discuss politics and business.", "Coffee reached Europe in the seventeenth century and quickly sparked both enthusiasm and alarm. Some European physicians warned that the drink was dangerous and should be avoided, while religious groups called for it to be banned. However, coffee's popularity proved impossible to suppress. Coffeehouses spread rapidly across England, France, and beyond, becoming important meeting places for writers, merchants, and intellectuals. Lloyd's of London, one of the world's most famous insurance markets, famously began as a coffeehouse. It was in these busy establishments that many significant business and political ideas of the era were first discussed and debated.", "Coffee's global spread was driven largely by colonial ambitions. European powers, recognizing the enormous commercial value of coffee, worked to establish plantations in their tropical colonies. The Dutch were among the first to cultivate coffee outside Arabia, successfully growing it in Java, Indonesia, in the early 1600s. The French later introduced coffee plants to the Caribbean, while the Portuguese brought cultivation to Brazil — a country that would eventually become the world's largest coffee producer. This expansion, however, came at a significant human cost, as coffee cultivation frequently relied on enslaved or exploited labor.", "From a scientific perspective, coffee's appeal lies primarily in its high caffeine content. Caffeine is a natural stimulant that blocks adenosine, a chemical in the brain that promotes feelings of tiredness. As a result, coffee drinkers typically experience increased alertness, improved concentration, and a temporary boost in energy levels. Research has also suggested that moderate coffee consumption may be associated with a range of health benefits, including a reduced risk of certain neurological conditions and type 2 diabetes. However, excessive consumption can lead to anxiety, disrupted sleep, and elevated heart rate, so health experts generally recommend limiting intake to around three to four cups per day.", "Today, coffee culture has evolved far beyond the simple act of brewing a hot drink. The rise of specialty coffee has introduced consumers to concepts such as single-origin beans, precise roasting profiles, and various brewing methods including pour-over, cold brew, and espresso. Baristas are now regarded in many circles as skilled artisans, and competitions celebrating their craft attract global audiences. Whether enjoyed as a morning ritual, a social occasion, or a sensory experience, coffee continues to hold a unique place in the daily lives of billions of people around the world."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Coffee is a dangerous beverage that has caused political and social conflicts throughout history.", "Coffee has a rich global history and continues to play a significant role in culture, science, and daily life.", "Coffee became popular worldwide mainly because of its proven health benefits and scientific properties.", "Coffee was first developed in Arabia and later spread to Europe through colonial trade routes."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the primary role of coffeehouses known as \"qahveh khaneh\" in the Middle East?", options: ["They were medical institutions where physicians studied the health effects of coffee on the human body.", "They served as busy social spaces where people met to share information, play games, and debate ideas.", "They were places where merchants stored and traded large quantities of coffee beans for export.", "They were religious centers where monks gathered to perform prayers and study ancient texts."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the example of Lloyd's of London illustrate?", options: ["It shows that European coffeehouses were primarily used by wealthy businessmen to avoid paying taxes.", "It suggests that French coffeehouses were more influential than those in England during the seventeenth century.", "It demonstrates that coffeehouses in Europe played an important role in shaping major institutions and ideas.", "It proves that the insurance industry was founded specifically to protect the interests of coffee traders."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which country became the first to successfully grow coffee outside of Arabia?", options: ["Brazil, where the Portuguese established large coffee plantations in tropical regions of South America.", "Turkey, where Ottoman merchants developed new farming techniques to grow coffee in non-native climates.", "France, where colonial authorities introduced coffee plants to Caribbean islands for commercial production.", "Indonesia, where Dutch colonizers successfully cultivated coffee in Java in the early seventeenth century."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the goats that became energetic after consuming berries from a particular tree in the Ethiopian highlands.", "the monks at the local monastery who drank the coffee and remained alert during long evening prayers.", "the red berries that were brought to the monastery and used to make an energizing beverage.", "the historians who debated whether the popular legend about Kaldi and the goats was actually true."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does caffeine affect the human brain?", options: ["It stimulates the release of adrenaline in the bloodstream, which causes the heart to beat significantly faster.", "It directly increases the production of adenosine in the brain, which creates feelings of alertness and focus.", "It prevents adenosine from functioning normally in the brain, which reduces tiredness and increases alertness.", "It blocks the production of dopamine in the brain, which improves concentration and boosts long-term memory."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"commercial\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["related to the cultural exchange between different civilizations during periods of exploration and discovery.", "related to the production of goods and the generation of profit through organized trade and business.", "related to the scientific research conducted by scholars to understand newly discovered natural resources.", "related to the political ambitions of governments seeking to expand their influence in distant territories."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"caffeine\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a synthetic compound added to beverages during manufacturing to enhance their flavor and increase consumer appeal.", "a naturally occurring substance in coffee that stimulates the brain and reduces the sensation of fatigue.", "a nutritional supplement derived from coffee plants that strengthens the immune system and prevents common illnesses.", "a natural chemical found in coffee that causes long-term damage to the nervous system when consumed regularly."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an effect of drinking coffee?", options: ["A temporary increase in the drinker's overall energy levels throughout the day.", "An immediate improvement in a person's long-term physical strength and endurance.", "An improvement in a person's ability to concentrate on tasks requiring mental focus.", "A reduction in the risk of developing certain neurological conditions over time."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's attitude toward coffee based on the passage as a whole?", options: ["The author argues that modern coffee culture has lost its historical importance and become overly commercialized.", "The author presents a balanced view, acknowledging both coffee's cultural significance and its associated risks.", "The author is critical of coffee's global spread because it caused significant harm to exploited workers in colonies.", "The author strongly promotes coffee consumption and believes its benefits far outweigh any potential negative effects."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Understanding Infant Sleep: Patterns, Challenges, and Development", paragraphs: ["Sleep is one of the most essential biological processes for human development, and nowhere is this more apparent than in infancy. In the first year of life, babies spend the majority of their time asleep — newborns may sleep between sixteen and eighteen hours a day — yet the nature of infant sleep differs considerably from adult sleep in ways that many parents find surprising and, at times, deeply challenging. Understanding the science behind infant sleep patterns can help caregivers respond more effectively to their child's needs while also managing their own expectations.", "Unlike adults, who move through predictable cycles of light and deep sleep approximately every ninety minutes, newborns operate on much shorter and less consolidated sleep cycles of around forty to fifty minutes. During each cycle, infants spend a significantly larger proportion of time in Rapid Eye Movement (REM) sleep compared to adults. REM sleep, often associated with dreaming in older individuals, is believed to play a critical role in early brain development, supporting the formation of neural connections and the processing of the enormous amount of sensory information that newborns absorb each day. This biological emphasis on REM sleep is thought to explain why infants wake so frequently — they are inherently designed to rouse between sleep cycles rather than seamlessly transition from one to the next as adults typically do.", "As infants grow, their sleep patterns gradually shift toward those of adults. By around three to four months of age, babies begin to consolidate their sleep into longer nighttime stretches, though this process is neither linear nor predictable. Many parents report that just as their baby appears to be sleeping through the night, a sudden regression occurs — periods of disrupted sleep that seem to undo weeks of progress. Developmental milestones such as learning to roll over, sit up, or pull to a standing position can temporarily interfere with sleep, as the brain practices new motor skills even during rest. Similarly, teething, illness, and changes in routine such as travel or a new caregiver can all cause significant disturbances in an infant's sleep schedule.", "Feeding behavior is closely linked to infant sleep, particularly in the early months. Newborns have tiny stomachs that can hold only small amounts of milk at a time, meaning they require frequent feeds — often every two to three hours — regardless of whether it is day or night. Breastfed babies may wake somewhat more frequently than formula-fed infants because breast milk is digested more quickly, though research consistently shows that both feeding methods support healthy infant development. As solid foods are gradually introduced from around six months of age, many infants begin to sleep for longer periods, though feeding remains one of the primary signals through which babies communicate their needs during the night.", "The sleep environment plays an equally important role in shaping infant sleep quality. Guidelines from major pediatric health organizations consistently recommend that infants sleep on their backs on a firm, flat surface, free from soft bedding, pillows, and toys that could pose suffocation risks. Room-sharing — placing the infant's crib or bassinet in the parents' bedroom — is encouraged for at least the first six months of life, as research suggests it significantly reduces the risk of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS). Temperature regulation is also a key consideration, as overheating has been identified as a contributing factor to SIDS. A comfortable, dark, and quiet environment, combined with consistent bedtime routines such as bathing, reading, or gentle singing, can help signal to the infant's developing brain that the time for sleep has arrived.", "Despite the inevitable difficulties of the early months, most children develop more stable sleep patterns by the end of their first year. Research in pediatric sleep science has helped parents distinguish between normal developmental variation and signs that may warrant professional consultation, such as persistent difficulty breathing during sleep or extreme resistance to settling at any hour. For the majority of families, patience, consistency, and a realistic understanding of infant biology remain the most valuable tools in navigating the challenging but ultimately temporary terrain of sleepless nights."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To explain the biological basis of infant sleep patterns and the various factors that influence sleep quality.", "To argue that parents should follow strict sleep training methods in order to establish healthy infant routines.", "To describe the most common sleep disorders diagnosed in infants during the first twelve months of life.", "To compare the sleep habits of breastfed and formula-fed infants and determine which method is more beneficial."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do infants spend more time in REM sleep than adults do?", options: ["REM sleep reduces the frequency of nighttime waking by allowing infants to transition smoothly between sleep cycles.", "REM sleep supports the rapid development of the brain by helping form neural connections and process new information.", "REM sleep allows infants to recover from the physical exertion of learning new motor skills throughout the day.", "REM sleep enables infants to practice feeding behaviors and strengthens the muscles needed for physical growth."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the research conducted by pediatric health organizations into the primary causes of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome.", "the practice of placing the infant's sleeping space inside the same bedroom as the parents for at least six months.", "the comfortable and dark environment that parents are advised to create in order to improve infant sleep quality.", "the firm and flat surface on which pediatric organizations recommend that all infants should be placed for sleep."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do newborns need to feed every two to three hours during the night?", options: ["Newborns have very small stomachs that cannot hold sufficient quantities of milk to sustain them for longer intervals.", "Newborns have underdeveloped digestive systems that are unable to absorb nutrients efficiently from either milk or formula.", "Newborns require frequent stimulation during the night to prevent them from entering excessively deep sleep cycles.", "Newborns are biologically programmed to wake at regular intervals because nighttime feeding supports brain development."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does research suggest about room-sharing in the first six months of an infant's life?", options: ["It causes infants to become overly dependent on parental proximity and makes independent sleep more difficult to achieve.", "It lowers the likelihood of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome occurring during the most vulnerable period of infancy.", "It allows parents to monitor feeding frequency more accurately and respond more quickly to signs of illness.", "It improves the quality of the infant's sleep by providing a familiar scent and sound that reduces nighttime anxiety."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, at approximately what age do infants begin sleeping for longer stretches at night?", options: ["From around six months of age, when solid foods are introduced and the infant's stomach capacity increases significantly.", "From birth, provided that parents establish a consistent bedtime routine involving bathing, reading, and gentle singing.", "From around twelve months of age, when the major developmental milestones of the first year have been fully completed.", "From around three to four months of age, when sleep patterns begin to consolidate into longer nighttime periods."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"consolidated\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["regulated automatically by the body's internal clock so that sleeping and waking occur at predictable fixed times.", "lengthened and joined together into longer and more continuous periods rather than remaining short and fragmented.", "separated into smaller and more frequent intervals that are distributed evenly throughout the day and night.", "deepened significantly in quality so that the brain receives a greater proportion of restorative slow-wave sleep."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"inherently\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporarily, suggesting that the behavior exists only during a brief phase and disappears as the infant matures.", "by nature, indicating that the characteristic is a fundamental and built-in feature rather than a learned response.", "accidentally, implying that the pattern developed without a clear biological purpose or evolutionary advantage.", "externally, indicating that the behavior is shaped entirely by environmental conditions rather than internal biology."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of sleep disruption in infants?", options: ["The process of teething, which can cause significant discomfort and interfere with an infant's ability to settle.", "Periods of illness, which can disturb an infant's regular sleep schedule during the months of early development.", "Excessive screen time in the hours before sleep, which overstimulates the infant's developing visual cortex.", "The introduction of a new caregiver or travel to an unfamiliar environment that disrupts established daily routines."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about infants who wake frequently during the night?", options: ["Frequent nighttime waking reflects a normal stage of neurological development rather than a problem requiring intervention.", "Frequent nighttime waking is most commonly caused by poor parenting choices and can be avoided with proper sleep training.", "Frequent nighttime waking is primarily driven by hunger and will stop completely once solid foods are regularly introduced.", "Frequent nighttime waking in infants is a reliable sign of an underlying medical condition that requires prompt diagnosis."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Mechanisms of Influence: How Social Pressure Shapes Human Behaviour", paragraphs: ["The capacity to be influenced by others is not a weakness of character but a fundamental feature of human social life. From the moment of birth, individuals are embedded in networks of relationships, norms, and expectations that subtly — and sometimes forcefully — shape the choices they make, the beliefs they hold, and the identities they construct. Social scientists have long sought to understand the mechanisms through which this influence operates, and their findings reveal a picture far more complex than the simple notion of \"peer pressure\" that dominates popular discourse. [1]", "One of the most well-documented mechanisms of social influence is conformity — the tendency to align one's behaviour or beliefs with those of a group. The psychologist Solomon Asch demonstrated this phenomenon in a now-famous series of experiments conducted in the 1950s, in which participants were asked to match the length of a line to one of three comparison lines. When confederates of the experimenter deliberately gave incorrect answers, a significant proportion of genuine participants also gave wrong answers, despite the evidence of their own senses. Asch's findings challenged the assumption that individuals are primarily rational agents who rely on objective evidence, suggesting instead that the desire for social acceptance can override accurate perception. [2]", "Beyond conformity, researchers have identified a distinct but related phenomenon known as normative influence , which operates through the implicit standards of acceptable behaviour within a social group. Unlike informational influence — where individuals adopt others' views because they believe those views to be correct — normative influence functions through the fear of social rejection or the desire for approval. This distinction has significant practical implications. Studies on energy conservation, for example, have found that informing residents how their energy consumption compares to that of their neighbours is more effective at reducing usage than providing information about environmental benefits alone. The mere knowledge that one's behaviour deviates from a social norm appears to be a powerful motivator for change. [3]", "The digital age has dramatically amplified the reach and speed of social influence. Social media platforms expose individuals to vast networks of peers, celebrities, and strangers whose behaviours and opinions are continuously visible and quantified through metrics such as likes, shares, and follower counts. Research by social psychologists suggests that this environment intensifies conformity pressures by making social comparisons more frequent, more immediate, and more public than at any previous point in human history. Paradoxically, the same platforms that claim to celebrate individuality and self-expression may in fact generate powerful homogenising forces, nudging users toward mainstream opinions and behaviours in ways that are difficult to detect or resist. [4]", "Authority represents another major channel through which influence is exercised, and its effects can be both constructive and deeply troubling. The psychologist Stanley Milgram's obedience experiments of the 1960s demonstrated that ordinary people would administer what they believed to be dangerous electric shocks to strangers simply because a figure in a white laboratory coat instructed them to do so. While Milgram's methodology has since been critiqued on ethical grounds, the underlying finding — that perceived authority can lead individuals to act in ways that contradict their personal values — has been replicated across numerous cultural contexts. This susceptibility to authority is not inherently irrational; in complex societies, deferring to experts and institutions often produces better outcomes than independent judgment. The danger arises when authority figures exploit this tendency for manipulative or harmful purposes.", "Resisting unwanted influence requires not merely willpower but a sophisticated understanding of the psychological mechanisms at play. Awareness of conformity pressures, for instance, can provide individuals with the cognitive distance needed to evaluate group behaviour critically rather than simply mirroring it. Exposure to minority viewpoints — even when those viewpoints are ultimately rejected — has been shown to enhance the quality of group decision-making by disrupting premature consensus. Educational programmes that teach media literacy and critical thinking skills are therefore increasingly regarded by researchers and policymakers alike as essential tools for cultivating populations capable of engaging with influence thoughtfully rather than reactively.", "Ultimately, the study of social influence is not merely an academic exercise. Its insights have direct applications in fields ranging from public health campaigns and political communication to marketing, education, and organisational leadership. Understanding how influence works — and how it can be both harnessed for social good and resisted when it threatens individual autonomy — is among the most practically urgent questions that the social sciences are currently equipped to address."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["Social influence operates through multiple psychological mechanisms and has wide-ranging implications for both individuals and society.", "Social influence is a destructive force that undermines individual reasoning and should be resisted through education.", "The experiments of Asch and Milgram conclusively proved that human beings are incapable of making truly independent decisions.", "Digital technology has made social influence far more dangerous than it was in previous historical periods."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What did Solomon Asch's line experiments primarily demonstrate about human behaviour?", options: ["That participants who were exposed to incorrect group answers eventually improved their perceptual accuracy over time.", "That individuals consistently rely on objective sensory evidence when making judgments, even under significant group pressure.", "That conformity to group norms is a behaviour unique to individuals with low self-esteem or limited cognitive ability.", "That the desire for social acceptance can lead people to give answers they know to be incorrect when others do the same."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the individuals who are responsible for transmitting normative expectations to newer members of a social community.", "the practical implications that researchers have identified when studying the mechanisms of social influence.", "the implicit standards of acceptable behaviour that are established and enforced within a particular social group.", "the views or opinions held by others that an individual adopts because they are believed to be accurate."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why was comparing residents' energy use to their neighbours more effective than providing environmental information?", options: ["Because environmental information was too complex for most residents to understand without professional guidance.", "Because the awareness of deviating from a local social norm created a stronger motivation to change behaviour than factual data alone.", "Because residents were more motivated by financial incentives than by abstract concerns about environmental sustainability.", "Because residents trusted information about their neighbours' behaviour more than they trusted official government statistics."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does Milgram's obedience research suggest about the role of perceived authority?", options: ["It suggests that authority figures are only effective in influencing behaviour when they use explicit rewards and punishments.", "It suggests that obedience to authority is a behaviour confined to specific cultural contexts and does not generalise globally.", "It suggests that individuals who follow authority figures are fundamentally less moral than those who resist institutional pressure.", "It suggests that perceived authority can cause ordinary individuals to act against their own values, though this tendency is not always irrational."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 4, what can be concluded about the relationship between social media and normative influence?", options: ["Social media weakens normative influence by exposing users to a wider diversity of opinions and reducing dependence on local group norms.", "Social media intensifies normative influence by making social comparisons more constant, visible, and quantified than in offline environments.", "Social media replaces normative influence with informational influence because users primarily seek accurate facts rather than social approval.", "Social media reduces the impact of normative influence because the anonymity of online spaces shields users from the fear of social rejection."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"amplified\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["fundamentally altered the psychological nature of social influence so that it now operates through different cognitive pathways.", "redirected the mechanisms of social influence toward entirely new populations that were previously unaffected by peer pressure.", "temporarily accelerated the spread of social influence during specific periods of heightened collective anxiety or uncertainty.", "significantly increased the scale and speed at which social influence is able to spread across human populations."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"conformity\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the gradual process through which social norms are negotiated and revised by members of a community over time.", "the tendency to adjust one's own behaviour or beliefs to match those of a surrounding social group.", "the automatic process by which individuals unconsciously absorb the emotional states of those around them.", "the deliberate strategy of adopting contrarian positions in order to distinguish oneself from the dominant group."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a field where insights from social influence research can be applied?", options: ["The design of public health campaigns intended to encourage healthier behaviours among general populations.", "The enhancement of organisational leadership practices within institutional and corporate environments.", "The development of political communication strategies used by governments and advocacy organisations.", "The improvement of individual athletic performance through targeted motivational coaching techniques."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'These results were particularly striking given that the correct answer was visually obvious to anyone examining the lines independently.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Viral Disease in the Modern Era: Ecology, Evolution, and the Limits of Control", paragraphs: ["The history of human civilisation is inextricably intertwined with the history of viral disease. From the smallpox epidemics that devastated ancient populations to the influenza pandemic of 1918 and the emergence of HIV/AIDS in the late twentieth century, viruses have repeatedly demonstrated their capacity to reshape societies, redraw demographic maps, and expose the fragility of even the most sophisticated medical infrastructures. [1] Yet despite centuries of accumulated knowledge and the remarkable advances of modern virology, the fundamental challenge posed by viral pathogens remains stubbornly resistant to resolution — a reality that the COVID-19 pandemic made viscerally apparent to a global audience previously accustomed to regarding infectious disease as a problem largely confined to history or to low-income settings.", "Viruses occupy a peculiar position in the taxonomy of life. Unlike bacteria, fungi, or parasites, they are not technically alive in the conventional biological sense: they lack the cellular machinery necessary for independent metabolism and can only reproduce by hijacking the molecular apparatus of a host cell. This obligate intracellular parasitism means that antiviral drug development is inherently more complex than antibiotic development, since any intervention that disrupts viral replication risks collateral damage to the host's own cellular processes. The relative scarcity of effective antiviral therapies compared to antibacterial agents reflects this fundamental biological constraint rather than any absence of scientific effort or investment.", "The ecological conditions underpinning viral emergence have become a central preoccupation of epidemiologists and conservation biologists alike. The majority of novel human pathogens are zoonotic in origin — that is, they arise in animal reservoirs and subsequently spill over into human populations through mechanisms that remain incompletely understood. Bats, in particular, have attracted intense scientific scrutiny as reservoirs for a disproportionately large number of highly pathogenic viruses, including the coronaviruses responsible for SARS, MERS, and COVID-19. The evolutionary explanation for this association is thought to involve the unique immunological adaptations that bats have developed in response to the energetic demands of powered flight, which may allow them to coexist with viral loads that would prove lethal in other mammalian species. Deforestation, agricultural expansion into previously wild habitats, and the intensification of the live animal trade have all been identified as drivers that increase the frequency of human-animal contact and thereby elevate the probability of spillover events.", "Once a novel virus establishes sustained human-to-human transmission, its evolutionary trajectory is shaped by the interplay between viral mutation rates and the selective pressures imposed by host immune responses. RNA viruses, which include influenza, HIV, and the coronaviruses, are characterised by exceptionally high mutation rates owing to the error-prone nature of RNA replication enzymes. This mutational plasticity enables rapid adaptation to new host environments and is the principal reason why RNA viruses dominate the landscape of emerging infectious disease. It also poses a formidable challenge to vaccine development: the influenza vaccine, for example, must be reformulated annually because the virus evolves sufficiently between seasons to render prior immunity partially obsolete. The emergence of antigenically distinct variants capable of partially evading existing immune responses — a phenomenon observed dramatically during the COVID-19 pandemic — illustrates how viral evolution can outpace the deployment of public health countermeasures even under conditions of intensive global surveillance.", "The governance of viral disease at the international level has long been characterised by a fundamental tension between the imperative of rapid, transparent information-sharing and the economic and reputational incentives that discourage states from promptly disclosing outbreaks. The International Health Regulations, which were substantially revised following the SARS outbreak of 2003, establish a legal framework obligating member states of the World Health Organisation to report events that may constitute a public health emergency of international concern. However, compliance with these obligations has been uneven, and the mechanisms available to the international community to compel timely disclosure remain limited. Critics have argued that the current architecture of global health governance is structurally ill-equipped to manage pathogens that spread across borders within hours of initial emergence, given the inherently national orientation of the institutions responsible for disease surveillance.", "Technological innovation has nonetheless transformed the landscape of viral disease response in ways that would have been inconceivable to earlier generations of epidemiologists. The development of mRNA vaccine technology — demonstrated at scale for the first time during the COVID-19 pandemic — compressed the timeline from pathogen identification to clinical vaccine deployment from years to months, representing arguably the most significant advance in vaccinology since the mid-twentieth century. Genomic sequencing capabilities have similarly revolutionised outbreak investigation, enabling researchers to reconstruct transmission chains, identify the geographic origins of novel strains, and detect the emergence of variants of concern with a speed and resolution previously unattainable. [2]", "Despite these advances, the structural determinants of viral disease burden — poverty, malnutrition, inadequate sanitation, and limited access to healthcare — remain profoundly unequally distributed across the global population. Technological solutions developed in high-income settings frequently fail to reach the communities most severely affected by viral outbreaks, not because the science is unavailable but because the political will and economic mechanisms required to ensure equitable distribution are absent. [3] The history of viral disease is therefore not only a history of biological processes but equally a history of the social and political conditions that determine which populations bear the greatest burden of suffering when those processes unfold. [4]"], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["International governance frameworks are the single most important factor determining whether viral outbreaks escalate into global pandemics.", "Viral diseases are primarily a scientific problem that can be resolved through sustained investment in vaccine technology and genomic surveillance systems.", "The threat posed by viral pathogens is rooted in biological complexity, ecological disruption, and structural inequalities that together resist straightforward resolution.", "RNA viruses pose a categorically greater danger to human populations than other pathogen types because of their uniquely high mutation rates."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is antiviral drug development more complex than antibiotic development?", options: ["Because antiviral research receives significantly less public funding than antibacterial research, limiting the pace of scientific discovery.", "Because viruses evolve far more rapidly than bacteria and can develop resistance to antiviral compounds within a single treatment course.", "Because viruses are capable of lying dormant inside host tissue for extended periods, making them difficult to target with conventional drugs.", "Because disrupting viral replication inside host cells risks causing damage to the host's own cellular structures and functions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the bats that have developed distinctive immune adaptations enabling coexistence with viral loads lethal to other mammals.", "the novel human pathogens that have emerged from animal reservoirs and established sustained transmission in human populations.", "the conservation biologists and epidemiologists who have studied the ecological conditions linked to viral spillover events.", "the coronaviruses responsible for SARS, MERS, and COVID-19 that have been identified in bat populations worldwide."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 4, what makes RNA viruses particularly difficult to control once they emerge in human populations?", options: ["RNA viruses originate exclusively in bat populations, which are ecologically inaccessible and therefore cannot be monitored by surveillance systems.", "RNA viruses are exclusively transmitted through direct physical contact, making conventional public health containment strategies largely ineffective.", "RNA viruses combine zoonotic origins with high mutation rates, allowing them to both enter human populations readily and rapidly adapt to evade immune responses.", "RNA viruses are undetectable by current genomic sequencing technologies until they have already established sustained transmission across multiple countries."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what fundamental problem does the author identify with international health governance frameworks?", options: ["International health governance is undermined by scientific disagreements between high-income and low-income member states about the definition of a public health emergency.", "The International Health Regulations were drafted before the emergence of RNA viruses and do not contain provisions relevant to modern pandemic threats.", "The institutions responsible for global disease surveillance are oriented primarily around national interests and lack adequate mechanisms to enforce timely outbreak disclosure.", "Member states of the World Health Organisation have consistently refused to ratify the revised International Health Regulations adopted after the SARS outbreak."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the author suggest is the most significant aspect of mRNA vaccine technology as demonstrated during the COVID-19 pandemic?", options: ["That it represented the first vaccine technology capable of generating sterilising immunity rather than merely reducing the severity of disease outcomes.", "That it dramatically reduced the time required to move from identifying a new pathogen to deploying a clinically approved vaccine against it.", "That it eliminated the need for annual vaccine reformulation by producing immune responses durable enough to neutralise multiple viral variants simultaneously.", "That it demonstrated a level of manufacturing scalability that allowed equitable global vaccine distribution to be achieved within twelve months of pathogen identification."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"plasticity\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the capacity of a biological system to change its form or function flexibly in response to new selective pressures.", "the structural rigidity of RNA replication enzymes that prevents the introduction of errors during viral reproduction.", "the stability of viral genomes that allows RNA viruses to maintain consistent antigenic properties across successive generations.", "the tendency of RNA viruses to revert to ancestral genetic sequences after periods of rapid evolutionary divergence."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"spill over\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to transfer from an animal reservoir into a human population through cross-species contact or transmission.", "to mutate sufficiently within a host organism to acquire the ability to cause more severe clinical symptoms.", "to exceed the containment capacity of public health systems during the early exponential phase of an outbreak.", "to spread uncontrollably within a single host species after reaching a critical threshold of viral replication."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT identified as a driver that increases the probability of viral spillover from animals to humans?", options: ["The expansion of farming activities into previously undisturbed wild habitats that serve as reservoirs for zoonotic pathogens.", "The growth of international air travel networks, which accelerates the geographic spread of pathogens after initial spillover has occurred.", "The intensification of trade in live animals, which facilitates close and repeated contact between humans and potential reservoir species.", "The clearing of forested land for agriculture or human settlement, which reduces the natural buffer between wildlife and human communities."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This disparity suggests that closing the gap between scientific capability and equitable access represents one of the defining public health challenges of the coming decades.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
